[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The CLOS fabric is healthy; nothing at the “top” (super_spine_router_0) is blackholing traffic. End-to-end reachability between racks is clean: pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 shows 0% loss with ~3 ms RTT both directions. At the core, super_spine_router_0 has both BGP adjacencies established to the spines and is receiving/advertising the correct prefixes (show ip bgp summary: both 172.16.0.1 and 172.16.0.3 up, 2 prefixes each). Its FIB contains routes to 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_0 (172.16.0.1), and both uplink interfaces show zero RX/TX errors or drops. Downstream, both spines have correct BGP-learned routes to each leaf (spine 0_0: 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.5, 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.7; spine 0_1: 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.9, 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.11), confirming forwarding paths are intact.

Mechanism: The super-spine currently selects a single best path per prefix (standard BGP bestpath), so both rack prefixes resolve via spine_router_0_0. That does not cause loss—spine_router_0_0 forwards to the correct leaf. If the expectation was traffic to use “both sides,” that would require ECMP, not a fault fix.

Localization: No drops or misroutes observed on super_spine_router_0 or the spines.

Root cause: User-perceived outage is not reproducible; the fabric forwards correctly and the super-spine is not suppressing traffic.

Corrective fix: No corrective action needed. If you want traffic split across both spines (“proper side”), enable eBGP multipath on super_spine (and optionally spines): address-family ipv4 unicast; maximum-paths 2; bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax. === END ===